Subject: [PATCH] ecmcExampleTop: link ethercat and ruckig after libecmc

`-lethercat` and `-lruckig` are added through `USR_LDFLAGS`, which EPICS emits
*before* the object files and before the `_LIBS`/`SYS_LIBS` flags:

    g++ -o ecmcIoc -Wl,-Bstatic ... -lethercat ... -lruckig ...
        ecmcIoc*.o -lecmc -lmotor -lexprtkSupport -lasyn ... -Wl,-Bdynamic ...

Under `STATIC_BUILD=YES` that is fatal.  A static archive contributes only the
symbols already demanded to its left, so `ld` scans `libethercat.a` and
`libruckig.a` while nothing has asked for anything, discards both, and then
reaches `libecmc.a` -- which needs them:

    libecmc.a(ecmcEc.o): undefined reference to `ecrt_release_master'
    libecmc.a(ecmcTrajectoryS.o): undefined reference to
      `ruckig::PositionSecondOrderStep1::get_profile(...)'

117 undefined references on this host, in exactly two families.

This is the same defect 0003 fixed for asyn, but `_LIBS` cannot reach these two
the same way.  `ecmcIoc_LIBS` tells EPICS "this is an EPICS-module library" and
it searches for it under this application's own `lib/<T_A>/` tree -- where
`libecmc.a` lives and `libethercat.a`/`libruckig.a` never will:

    make: *** No rule to make target '../../../lib/rhel9-x86_64/libethercat.a'

`ecmcIoc_SYS_LIBS` is the right list for a library that already has its own
`-L`: EPICS simply emits `-l<name>`, with no search, placed after
`-Wl,-Bdynamic` -- still after `-lecmc`, which is what resolves the undefined
references.  The `-L` and `-Wl,-rpath` flags stay in `USR_LDFLAGS`, unchanged:
only `-l` position matters to archive resolution.

Two details the file dictates, both easy to miss:

  * The RUCKIG block sits OUTSIDE the arch conditional, so `-lruckig` reached the
    link line even on a build where the `linux-%` filter missed and the whole
    ETHERLAB block was skipped.  It needs its own hunk.
  * BOTH branches of the conditional add `-lethercat`.  Patching only the native
    branch leaves a Yocto cross-build broken in a different way.

Like 0003, this is correct under either linking mode -- a dependency belongs
after its dependent regardless -- and only a site building statically ever sees
the failure.  Worth reporting upstream.

Depends on 0003 having reordered `_LIBS`; `apply-patches.sh` applies them in
numeric order, so the context here is the file with 0002 and 0003 already in.

Applies to: ecmc v11.0.8, after 0002 and 0003
Verified on: EPICS base R7.0.3.1-2.0, STATIC_BUILD=YES, rhel9-x86_64
---
 ecmcExampleTop/ecmcIocApp/src/Makefile | 12 ++++++++----
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

diff --git a/ecmcExampleTop/ecmcIocApp/src/Makefile b/ecmcExampleTop/ecmcIocApp/src/Makefile
--- a/ecmcExampleTop/ecmcIocApp/src/Makefile
+++ b/ecmcExampleTop/ecmcIocApp/src/Makefile
@@ -19,18 +19,16 @@
 USR_INCLUDES += -I$(ETHERLAB)/include
 USR_CFLAGS += -fPIC
 USR_LDFLAGS += -L$(ETHERLAB)/lib
-USR_LDFLAGS += -lethercat
 USR_LDFLAGS += -Wl,-rpath=$(ETHERLAB)/lib
 else
 USR_INCLUDES += -I$(SDKTARGETSYSROOT)/usr/include/etherlab
 USR_CFLAGS   += -fPIC
 USR_LDFLAGS  += -L$(SDKTARGETSYSROOT)/usr/lib/etherlab
-USR_LDFLAGS  += -lethercat
 USR_LDFLAGS  += -Wl,-rpath=$(SDKTARGETSYSROOT)/usr/lib/etherlab
 endif
 
 USR_INCLUDES += -I$(RUCKIG)/include/ruckig
-USR_LDFLAGS += -L$(RUCKIG)/build -lruckig
+USR_LDFLAGS += -L$(RUCKIG)/build
 USR_LDFLAGS += -Wl,-rpath,'$(RUCKIG)/build'
 
 # ecmcioc.dbd will be created and installed
@@ -50,8 +50,14 @@
 ecmcIoc_LIBS += motor
 ecmcIoc_LIBS += exprtkSupport
 ecmcIoc_LIBS += asyn
-ecmcIoc_LIBS += ethercat
-ecmcIoc_LIBS += ruckig
+
+# Not ecmcIoc_LIBS: that tells EPICS to look for an EPICS-module library
+# under this application's own lib/<T_A>/ tree, which is where libecmc.a
+# lives and libethercat.a/libruckig.a never will. SYS_LIBS just emits
+# -l<name> without that search, and EPICS places it after -Wl,-Bdynamic --
+# still after -lecmc, still resolving the same undefined references.
+ecmcIoc_SYS_LIBS += ethercat
+ecmcIoc_SYS_LIBS += ruckig
 
 ecmcIoc_SRCS += ecmcIoc_registerRecordDeviceDriver.cpp
 
